# DEPRECATED: Migrated to .cursor/rules/
# These rules have been migrated to Project Rules. See .cursor/rules/ for the current rules.
# This file is kept for reference only and can be removed.

project_name: PioneerDJCorrector

description: >
  Coding standards for the PioneerDJCorrector project.
  All code must adhere to these principles for consistency, maintainability, and quality.

rules:
  - name: Clean, Reusable, Modular Code
    description: >
      All code should be organized in small, single-purpose functions and components with clear names.
      Prefer composition over inheritance. Reuse utilities and patterns. New code should be generic enough to be reused.

  - name: No Comments in Code
    description: >
      Code must be self-explanatory through clear naming, small scopes, and obvious control flow.
      Replace comments with better function names, variables, or helpers.
      Record rationale in docs/technical_decisions.md, not source files.

  - name: Linting and Formatting
    description: >
      Formatter and linter must be run automatically on every change.
      Fix all warnings and errors. Never disable rules unless documented in technical_decisions.md.

  - name: Strict Typing
    description: >
      Use precise types, generics, and discriminated unions. Never use "any" or weak types.
      Prefer type guards and exhaustiveness checks.
      Public APIs must always have full types.

  - name: No Code Bloat
    description: >
      Code should be lean and purposeful. Remove obsolete code promptly.
      Avoid speculative abstractions. Keep dependencies minimal and justified.

  - name: No Duplication of Logic
    description: >
      Extract shared utilities or components for similar behavior. Favor DRY across modules, styles, and config.
      Refactor toward a single source of truth.

  - name: No Unnecessary Files
    description: >
      Do not add placeholder files or throwaway debug artifacts.
      Temporary helpers must be removed immediately after use.

  - name: Centralize Configuration and Globals
    description: >
      Keep shared constants and settings in a single file/folder.
      Never hardcode values – always reference config tokens.

  - name: Translations Belong in Their Own Place
    description: >
      Store translations/i18n in a dedicated folder with namespace keys.
      Do not inline user-facing text in code.

  - name: Documentation in docs/
    description: >
      Maintain docs/project.md for description and docs/technical_decisions.md for architectural choices.
      Document every non-obvious decision with context, date, and rationale.

  - name: README Contents
    description: >
      README.md should only include a short project description, how to run/build, and how to develop.
      Put deeper details in docs/project.md and docs/technical_decisions.md.

  - name: Run Commands in Bulk
    description: >
      Prefer chained scripts (e.g., npm run format && npm run lint). Add combined scripts if missing.

  - name: Edit Carefully
    description: >
      Read and understand files before editing. Preserve working behavior unless change is agreed.
      Refactor in minimal, focused changes.

  - name: Propose and Research Before Building
    description: >
      Outline options and research tradeoffs before any feature or major change.
      Never implement solutions known to fail; consult users first.

  - name: Technology Choices Come with Options
    description: >
      Always present at least two tech/library options with pros, cons, and constraints.
      Justify your choice and note exit strategies.

  - name: Dependencies Must Be Current
    description: >
      Prefer actively maintained packages. Identify outdated/deprecated dependencies, propose upgrades, and remove unused packages.

  - name: Consistent Architecture and Naming
    description: >
      Follow folder structure, naming conventions, and module boundaries.
      Keep public APIs stable and documented.

  - name: Single Responsibility and Clear Boundaries
    description: >
      Ensure each module/component does one thing well.
      Separate domain logic, presentation, configuration, and side-effects.

  - name: Explicit Error Handling
    description: >
      Handle errors explicitly where they occur; return useful error data to callers.
      Avoid silent failures. Validate all inputs. Keep error messages concise.

  - name: Performance by Design
    description: >
      Avoid unnecessary renders or allocations. Use efficient data structures. Cache if appropriate.
      Measure before further optimizations.

  - name: Testing Policy
    description: >
      Only add tests that provide real value and fit existing conventions. Avoid unused scaffolding.

  - name: Backward Compatibility
    description: >
      Document migration notes in docs/technical_decisions.md when changing public interfaces.
      Consider deprecation windows and add adapters where feasible.

  - name: Security and Privacy Basics
    description: >
      Never log secrets. Validate all untrusted inputs. Document assumptions and risks for auth/data.

  - name: Observability Without Clutter
    description: >
      Use logging and metrics utilities purposefully. Remove excessive logs once stable.

  - name: PR and Review Readiness
    description: >
      Produce focused PRs/commits with intent, scope, and risks.
      All code must pass format, lint, type checks, and tests before review.

adherence: >
  Adhere to all rules on every task.
  If a rule must be broken, explicitly call out why and record rationale/impact in docs/technical_decisions.md.
